## LIGHT demo — Sun + Moon, Minneapolis, 2026

Two bodies × 365 days. This fans out over all your BEAM schedulers via
`Task.async_stream` — each day is independent (its own noon frame + analytic
seeds + bracketed refinement), so the work distributes cleanly.

```elixir
minneapolis = %{lat: 44.9778, lon: -93.2650, height: 250.0}

{us, result} =
  :timer.tc(fn ->
    Almanac.generate(
      year: 2026,
      observer: minneapolis,
      bodies: [:sun, :moon]
    )
  end)

Kino.Markdown.new("""
**#{result.meta.event_count} events** (sun + moon, #{result.meta.year}) \
in **#{Float.round(us / 1_000_000, 2)} s** \
on #{System.schedulers_online()} schedulers.

Observer: `#{result.meta.location.latitude_deg}°, #{result.meta.location.longitude_deg}°` \
(#{result.meta.location.elevation_m} m)

Engine: `#{result.meta.engine}` · Kernel: `#{result.meta.kernel}` · \
Horizon: `#{result.meta.horizon_convention}`
""")
```

### Meta map

```elixir
result.meta
```

### First 20 events

```elixir
result.events
|> Enum.take(20)
|> then(event_rows)
|> Kino.DataTable.new(name: "First 20 rise/transit/set events")
```

## Event shape

Each event is a flat map. Rise and set events carry `az_deg` (azimuth at the
horizon crossing); transit events also carry `alt_deg` (culmination altitude).
Missing keys are real — `event_rows/1` (helpers cell) pads them to `nil` so
`Kino.DataTable` gets uniform columns. The `utc` string is truncated to seconds;
`tt_jd` is the precise Terrestrial-Time Julian date used internally.

```elixir
rise = Enum.find(result.events, &(&1.event == "rise"))
transit = Enum.find(result.events, &(&1.event == "transit"))
set = Enum.find(result.events, &(&1.event == "set"))

[rise, transit, set]
|> then(event_rows)
|> Kino.DataTable.new(name: "One rise / transit / set triple")
```

| Field     | Type   | Present on   | Notes                                 |
| --------- | ------ | ------------ | ------------------------------------- |
| `body`    | string | all          | `"sun"`, `"moon"`, `"mars"`, …      |
| `event`   | string | all          | `"rise"` / `"transit"` / `"set"`      |
| `utc`     | string | all          | ISO 8601, second-truncated            |
| `tt_jd`   | float  | all          | TT Julian date — internal precision |
| `az_deg`  | float  | all          | Azimuth at event (°)                 |
| `alt_deg` | float  | transit only | Culmination altitude (°)             |

## CPU — skip in dry-run

Classic-7 bodies (Sun, Moon, Mercury, Venus, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn) for a full
year. Expect 10–30 s depending on your machine. This exercises the full BEAM
fan-out story: 365 days × 7 bodies, each day independent, no serialization on
any process — the shared SPK ETS table is read-concurrent.

Tip: in Livebook, convert this section to a **branching section** so it does not
block evaluating the rest of the notebook.

```elixir
{heavy_us, heavy} =
  :timer.tc(fn ->
    Almanac.generate(
      year: 2026,
      observer: minneapolis,
      bodies: Almanac.preset(:classic7)
    )
  end)

Kino.Markdown.new("""
**#{heavy.meta.event_count} events** (classic7, #{heavy.meta.year}) \
in **#{Float.round(heavy_us / 1_000_000, 2)} s** \
on #{System.schedulers_online()} schedulers.

Bodies: #{Enum.join(heavy.meta.bodies, ", ")}
""")
```

```elixir
heavy.events
|> Enum.take(30)
|> then(event_rows)
|> Kino.DataTable.new(name: "Classic-7 — first 30 events")
```

## Concurrency notes

The almanac is embarrassingly parallel by UTC day: each day computes one noon
`ApparentPlace.frame`, runs the Meeus seed formula per body, brackets and
refines each crossing independently. `Task.async_stream` fans out over
`System.schedulers_online()` — on an 8-core laptop that is 8× throughput with
zero shared mutable state.

### What happens inside `events/4`

```
365 UTC days
  └─ Task.async_stream (max_concurrency: schedulers_online)
       └─ per day:
            1. ApparentPlace.frame(noon)          ← nutation, Earth velocity
            2. for body <- bodies:
                 ApparentPlace.look(noon_frame, body) ← RA/Dec
                 Meeus cos H₀ → analytic rise/transit/set seeds
                 bracket ±40 min, confirm sign change
                 solve_bracket → ≤ 250 µs tolerance
            3. collect events
  └─ dedupe_close_events → sort by tt_jd
```

The SPK kernel lives in a read-only ETS table — no GenServer, no serialization.
Each task does its own floating-point work on its own scheduler.

### Accuracy vs Skyfield

The target is **≤ 1 second** against Skyfield's `almanac()` for the same
observer, year, and body set. The analytic-seed approach replaces a dense
1-hour altitude grid (8 760 frames for the coarse sweep) with ~365 noon frames +
~10 evaluations per event — far cheaper while staying inside the accuracy
contract.

| Metric             | EphCore Almanac                | Skyfield `almanac()`  |
| ------------------ | ------------------------------ | --------------------- |
| Frame model        | IAU 2006/2000A (ApparentPlace) | IAU 2006/2000A        |
| Horizon convention | USNO thresholds                | USNO thresholds       |
| Accuracy target    | ≤ 1 s                        | reference             |
| Coarse grid        | analytic seeds (none)          | altitude every ~1 min |
| Concurrency        | `Task.async_stream`            | single-threaded NumPy |
| Language           | Elixir / BEAM                  | Python / C            |

## Key ideas

1. **One function, one year** — `Almanac.generate/1` returns rise/transit/set
   events for every day in the requested year.

2. **Analytic seeds** — Meeus hour-angle formula gives a ~minute-accurate first
   guess. Bracket ±40 min, confirm sign change, refine with inverse interpolation
   to 250 µs. No dense grid.

3. **BEAM fan-out** — each UTC day is independent; `Task.async_stream` scales
   with `System.schedulers_online()`.

4. **Cross-check** — compare against Skyfield via
   [`02_skyfield_snapshot.livemd`](02_skyfield_snapshot.livemd) or run
   `mix eph.snapshot_sanity_check` in dev.

